WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.029
I want to let you know today that, uh, there's
an increasingly desperate caravan,

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:12.929
people who have decided they simply cannot
stay in their home countries anymore economic

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:18.010
conditions and the sort of realities on the
ground are pushing them across the border

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:23.460
once and for all from the United States into
Canada where they can fall, hopefully find

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.560
cheaper healthcare and cheaper medications
than in the United States.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.640
Uh, there was a story some months ago I did
about a daily caravan of Americans that crosses

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.730
the southern border into Mexico for inexpensive
but good quality dentistry in Mexico.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:41.570
And I think also it was a optometry in addition
to dentistry.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:45.339
And there's a new report this week in Newsweek
about a so called caravan.

00:00:45.339 --> 00:00:49.989
And I'm using the term sort of satirically
people, Americans who are crossing into Canada

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:53.480
looking for more affordable health care for
type one diabetes.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:01:00.219
Uh, because the same drugs that are used to
treat diabetes in the United States are used

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:01.219
in Canada.

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:03.670
Uh, and they're much cheaper in Canada.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:10.070
Insulin can be purchased in Canada for about
a 10% of what it costs in the United States.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:15.050
And at some of, you know, since the mid nineties,
the price of insulin has increased more than

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:20.010
1100% according to data from Truven health
analytics.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:26.090
But as you also probably know, wages have
not increased 1100% since the mid nineties.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.470
And this is of course, in addition to what
most people are paying kind of in total for

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:36.850
their health care, which is health insurance,
premiums and copays and uncovered medical

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.880
expenses, et Cetera, et cetera.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:46.720
Now the FDA, um, is officially taking the
position that it is risky to travel outside

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.860
the United States for insulin because the
safety of the insulin might be compromised.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.320
The effectiveness of foreign insulin might
not be as high.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.910
And of course that could apply to some countries,
but in Canada it's fine.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:07.430
And this is just a truly horrible reality
and an optional problem of the system that

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:08.430
we have, right?

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:16.659
The profit motive and the political impasse
has fixed this issue and it prevents us from

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:17.909
actually dealing with it.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:19.749
And it's merely a decision we have to make.

00:02:19.749 --> 00:02:23.969
I mean we, we know, uh, exactly how this can
be dealt with.

00:02:23.969 --> 00:02:30.969
The, these are relatively simple math problems,
actuarial problems and mostly of political

00:02:30.969 --> 00:02:31.969
will.

00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:33.371
Um, and I guess this is a good opportunity.

00:02:33.371 --> 00:02:38.990
I mean, while we're at it, I should dispel
the notion that health care around the world

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.609
is terrible and unreliable and dangerous.

00:02:41.609 --> 00:02:44.780
It's not exactly what the FDA is saying when
they say you should go to gotta.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.819
Be really careful if you go anywhere else
for insulin.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:55.250
Obviously there are many places in the world
where I would try to avoid having to get healthcare

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.760
services, uh, if traveling or whatever the
case may be.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.269
But there are so many countries, even countries
considered so called third world or developing

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:09.150
countries often have very good health coverage
at very, very reasonable rates.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.930
Belize is an example.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.680
Belize has plenty of problems.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:19.879
The national income is about 8,000 PPP dollars,
a purchasing power parity dollars.

00:03:19.879 --> 00:03:23.069
So, um, uh, we don't even have to get to what
that means.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.760
Just think about 8,000 US dollars.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.790
Is that the sort of uh, um, national income
per person?

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:32.109
It's not a rich country, but care is very
good.

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:37.239
And many Americans retire in Belize, partially
because they can get good, cheap healthcare.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:44.169
Argentina, my home country riddled with problems,
I mean NASA of economic problems, but at private

00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:49.919
hospitals and private clinics where you would
be paying for care as a visiting American,

00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:54.790
uh, it is extraordinarily cheap by our standards
and you still get good care.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:58.590
Some of the public hospitals in Argentina,
particularly outside of the Capitol City,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:04.390
not the best, but for what we're used to paying,
going to private hospitals in Argentina would

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.799
be very accessible and very good.

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:07.799
Even in Mexico.

00:04:07.799 --> 00:04:13.209
Mexico is of interest more and more, uh, for
Americans to retire to and for medical tourism.

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:17.940
And a lot of websites now are solely dedicated
to how can you legally get resident status

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:18.940
in Mexico.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:25.250
It's of course, very stratified between regions
in Mexico in terms of what is available, but

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:30.080
really excellent hospitals and care and many
parts of the country, including Mexico City.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:36.449
So in any case, the American economy would
be better off if American's didn't feel the

00:04:36.449 --> 00:04:41.199
need to go to other countries for care because
you'd be keeping those dollars here.

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:45.129
You'd be funding people's salaries here in
the United States.

00:04:45.129 --> 00:04:51.889
Fortunately for us, Mexico and Canada are
welcoming to people from other countries who

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:56.150
come from countries that have third world
health insurance systems like we have in the

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:57.199
United States.

00:04:57.199 --> 00:04:58.419
And we know how to fix it.

00:04:58.419 --> 00:04:59.419
Okay.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:00.850
We have plans for how it can be done.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:05.289
We know what it would cost, but we remain
stuck in this decades long.

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:09.880
I mean, it's really like a century long impasse,
which dates back to the period between World

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:16.479
War One and World War II, where we were starting
to go in the direction of a more European

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:18.349
model on healthcare.

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:23.300
And then that idea was smeared as being too
much like Germany at a time when we weren't

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.040
getting along with Germany for good reasons,
by the way.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.419
Uh, and it's been downhill ever since.

00:05:28.419 --> 00:05:35.940
And you know where we are today in 2019 but
at least Canada, is there a for type one diabetes

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.820
sufferers to go and get inexpensive and still
reliable quality insulin.

